# Almost Everywhere Discontinuity in the Context of Lebesgue Measure

## Setup and Definitions

Let $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a function, and let $m$ denote the Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb{R}$.

**Definition (Point of continuity).** A point $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}$ is a *point of continuity* of $f$ if $f$ is continuous at $x_0$ in the usual $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ sense:

$$\forall \varepsilon > 0,\ \exists \delta > 0,\ \forall x:\ |x - x_0| < \delta \implies |f(x) - f(x_0)| < \varepsilon.$$

A point that is not a point of continuity is called a *point of discontinuity*.

**Definition (Almost everywhere discontinuous).** A function $f$ is *almost everywhere discontinuous* (with respect to Lebesgue measure) if the set of points at which $f$ is continuous has Lebesgue measure zero. Equivalently, letting

$$C(f) = \{x \in \mathbb{R} : f \text{ is continuous at } x\},$$

we say $f$ is almost everywhere discontinuous when

$$m\bigl(C(f)\bigr) = 0.$$

## Unpacking the Meaning

The phrase "almost everywhere" is a measure-theoretic qualifier. A property holds *almost everywhere* (a.e.) with respect to a measure $\mu$ if the set on which it fails has $\mu$-measure zero. Here:

- The property in question is **discontinuity**.
- The measure is **Lebesgue measure** $m$.
- "Almost everywhere discontinuous" means: discontinuity holds at $m$-almost every point, i.e., the exceptional set (the continuity set $C(f)$) is $m$-negligible.

So the statement "$f$ is almost everywhere discontinuous" asserts:

$$m\bigl(\{x : f \text{ is continuous at } x\}\bigr) = 0.$$

The set of continuity points, while it may be nonempty (even uncountable, e.g., a measure-zero Cantor-type set), is so "thin" from the Lebesgue-measure perspective that it carries no length.

## Key Structural Fact: The Baire/Oscillation Characterization

To make the definition non-vacuous and to connect it to the structure of $C(f)$, recall the **oscillation characterization**. For $x \in \mathbb{R}$, define the oscillation of $f$ at $x$:

$$\omega_f(x) = \inf_{\delta > 0}\ \sup\bigl\{|f(y) - f(z)| : y, z \in (x-\delta, x+\delta)\bigr\}.$$

Then $f$ is continuous at $x$ if and only if $\omega_f(x) = 0$. Consequently

$$C(f) = \{x : \omega_f(x) = 0\} = \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} \{x : \omega_f(x) < 1/n\}.$$

For an arbitrary function $f$, each set $\{x : \omega_f(x) < 1/n\}$ is open, so $C(f)$ is a **$G_\delta$ set** (a countable intersection of open sets). This is a classical fact:

> *The set of continuity points of any function $\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ is a $G_\delta$ set.*

Therefore, saying $f$ is almost everywhere discontinuous is the same as saying that this particular $G_\delta$ set $C(f)$ has Lebesgue measure zero.

## Canonical Example: The Dirichlet Function (Thomae Contrast)

**Dirichlet function.** Define

$$\mathbf{1}_{\mathbb{Q}}(x) = \begin{cases} 1, & x \in \mathbb{Q},\\ 0, & x \notin \mathbb{Q}. \end{cases}$$

This function is discontinuous at *every* point: at any $x_0$, every neighborhood contains both rationals (where the value is $1$) and irrationals (where the value is $0$), so $\omega_{\mathbf{1}_{\mathbb{Q}}}(x_0) = 1$ for all $x_0$. Hence $C(\mathbf{1}_{\mathbb{Q}}) = \varnothing$ and $m(C(\mathbf{1}_{\mathbb{Q}})) = 0$. The Dirichlet function is almost everywhere discontinuous — in fact, *everywhere* discontinuous, which is the strongest possible instance.

**Thomae's function (contrast).** Define

$$T(x) = \begin{cases} 1/q, & x = p/q \in \mathbb{Q},\ \gcd(p,q)=1,\ q>0,\\ 0, & x \notin \mathbb{Q}. \end{cases}$$

Thomae's function is continuous at every irrational and discontinuous at every rational. Thus $C(T) = \mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Q}$, and since $m(\mathbb{Q}) = 0$,

$$m\bigl(\mathbb{R}\setminus C(T)\bigr) = m(\mathbb{Q}) = 0.$$

So Thomae's function is discontinuous only on a measure-zero set — it is continuous *almost everywhere*, the opposite of our notion. This contrast clarifies that "almost everywhere discontinuous" is a genuine and nontrivial condition: it rules out functions like Thomae's, whose discontinuity set is negligible.

## Relationship to Riemann Integrability (Contextual Significance)

The notion gains practical force from **Lebesgue's criterion for Riemann integrability**:

> *A bounded function on $[a,b]$ is Riemann integrable if and only if its set of discontinuities has Lebesgue measure zero.*

Equivalently, a bounded $f$ on $[a,b]$ is Riemann integrable iff $f$ is continuous almost everywhere. Therefore:

- A bounded function that is **almost everywhere discontinuous** on $[a,b]$ has $m(C(f)\cap[a,b]) = 0$, i.e., its discontinuity set has *full measure* $m([a,b]) = b-a$. By Lebesgue's criterion, such a function is **not Riemann integrable**.
- The Dirichlet function on $[0,1]$ is the archetypal example: its discontinuity set is all of $[0,1]$, of measure $1$, so it fails Riemann integrability — even though it is Lebesgue integrable (with integral $0$, since it equals $0$ a.e. on $[0,1]$ because $m(\mathbb{Q}\cap[0,1])=0$).

This illustrates a key point: "almost everywhere discontinuous" is a statement about the **size** of the continuity set in the sense of Lebesgue measure, and it directly governs whether classical (Riemann) integration theory applies. The Lebesgue theory itself is more forgiving — it only cares about behavior up to measure-zero modifications, so a function can be almost everywhere discontinuous yet still be perfectly well-behaved as a Lebesgue-integrable function (as the Dirichlet function shows, being a.e. equal to the constant $0$).

## Precise Statement (Summary)

Putting everything together, the precise meaning is:

> **A function $f$ is almost everywhere discontinuous (with respect to Lebesgue measure) if and only if its set of continuity points $C(f) = \{x : \omega_f(x) = 0\}$ is a Lebesgue-null set, i.e., $m(C(f)) = 0$. Equivalently, the set of discontinuity points $\{x : \omega_f(x) > 0\}$ has full measure: its complement has measure zero. Since $C(f)$ is always a $G_\delta$ set, this is the statement that a certain $G_\delta$ set associated to $f$ has Lebesgue measure zero. For bounded $f$ on an interval, this is exactly the condition that fails Lebesgue's criterion, so such an $f$ is not Riemann integrable.**

The qualifier "in the context of Lebesgue measure" specifies that the notion of "almost everywhere" — and hence of "negligible exceptional set" — is taken with respect to Lebesgue measure $m$, as opposed to some other measure (e.g., a Dirac measure, a Hausdorff measure, or a probability measure). Under a different reference measure, the same function could be "almost everywhere continuous" or "almost everywhere discontinuous" depending on which sets that measure regards as negligible; the Lebesgue-measure context fixes the standard of "smallness" as having zero length.

